// GENERATED by scripts/build-research.mjs from research/*.json — do not edit by hand.
window.LM_RESEARCH = {"Melanotan II":{"grokipedia":"https://grokipedia.com/page/Melanotan_II","overview":"Melanotan II (MT-II) is a synthetic cyclic peptide analogue of alpha-melanocyte-stimulating hormone. It was developed in the 1980s at the University of Arizona as part of efforts to produce a sunless tanning agent, and it was also noticed to affect sexual arousal, which led to the later development of the related compound bremelanotide.\n\nIt has never been approved as a medicine in major jurisdictions, yet it is widely sold through gray-market vendors as a injectable or nasal-spray tanning product and is discussed heavily in looksmaxxing communities as a way to darken skin and reduce sunburn risk. Regulators such as the UK MHRA and Australia's TGA have issued warnings against its use, and it should not be confused with the prescription drug afamelanotide.","mechanism":"MT-II is a non-selective agonist at several melanocortin receptors. Activation of MC1R on melanocytes shifts melanin synthesis toward eumelanin and darkens the skin, while activity at MC3R and MC4R in the brain is thought to account for effects on appetite, libido and spontaneous erections. Because it acts systemically rather than only on the skin, its effects are not limited to pigmentation.","risks":"Reported adverse effects include nausea, flushing, fatigue and unwanted erections, and there are case reports of priapism and renal infarction. Case reports and small series describe new or changing moles, including atypical naevi and several melanomas in users, although causation has not been established and users often had other risk factors. Products are unregulated, so purity, sterility and actual content are unverified. Evidence is limited to small early trials, case reports and anecdote; there are no long-term safety data.","scholarly":[{"title":"Melanotan II: a possible cause of renal infarction: review of the literature and case report","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7148395/","source":"PubMed Central","year":2020},{"title":"Atypical melanocytic naevi following melanotan injection","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23914578/","source":"PubMed","year":null},{"title":"An overview of benefits and risks of chronic melanocortin-1 receptor activation","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11664455/","source":"PubMed Central","year":null},{"title":"Melanotan II nasal spray: a possible risk factor for oral mucosal malignant melanoma?","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40210573/","source":"PubMed","year":2025}],"forums":[{"title":"Melanotan-2 Guide (Inspired by MPMD)","url":"https://looksmax.org/threads/melanotan-2-guide-inspired-by-mpmd.1686405/","site":"looksmax.org"},{"title":"Updated Melanotan-2 Guide (Inspired by MPMD)","url":"https://looksmax.org/threads/updated-melanotan-2-guide-inspired-by-mpmd.1718108/","site":"looksmax.org"},{"title":"Melanotan 1 & 2 Peptide Guide/Walkthrough","url":"https://looksmax.org/threads/melanotan-1-2-peptide-guide-walkthrough.496758/","site":"looksmax.org"}],"image":null,"imageCredit":null},"Afamelanotide":{"grokipedia":"https://grokipedia.com/page/Afamelanotide","overview":"Afamelanotide, also called Melanotan I and sold as Scenesse, is a synthetic analogue of alpha-melanocyte-stimulating hormone. It is delivered as a subcutaneous implant and is approved in the European Union and the United States for adults with erythropoietic protoporphyria (EPP), a rare inherited disorder in which sunlight causes severe pain.\n\nIts relevance to looksmaxxing comes from the fact that it darkens skin, and it is sometimes treated as the legitimate sibling of gray-market Melanotan II. The strong evidence applies only to its approved medical use; it is a prescription product for a specific disease, not a cosmetic tanning option.","mechanism":"Afamelanotide is a relatively selective agonist at the melanocortin-1 receptor on melanocytes. Stimulating MC1R increases production of eumelanin, the photoprotective brown-black pigment, and may also support DNA repair and antioxidant responses. In EPP the increased pigmentation and related effects allow patients to tolerate more light exposure before symptoms appear.","risks":"In clinical trials adverse events were generally mild, with headache, nausea and implant-site reactions most often reported; skin darkening and changes in existing moles are expected effects, so regular skin checks are advised. It is available only by prescription through specialist centres and is costly. It should not be treated as evidence that unregulated melanotan products are safe.","scholarly":[{"title":"Phase III Confirmatory Study in Erythropoietic Protoporphyria (EPP) (NCT00979745)","url":"https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT00979745","source":"ClinicalTrials.gov","year":null},{"title":"Phase III Confirmatory Study in Erythropoietic Protoporphyria (NCT01605136)","url":"https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT01605136","source":"ClinicalTrials.gov","year":null},{"title":"Phase 3 afamelanotide trials in erythropoietic protoporphyria (PMC full text)","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4780255","source":"PubMed Central","year":2015}],"forums":[{"title":"Melanotan 1 & 2 Peptide Guide/Walkthrough","url":"https://looksmax.org/threads/melanotan-1-2-peptide-guide-walkthrough.496758/","site":"looksmax.org"}],"image":null,"imageCredit":null},"Dihydroxyacetone":{"grokipedia":"https://grokipedia.com/page/Dihydroxyacetone","overview":"Dihydroxyacetone (DHA) is a simple three-carbon sugar and the active ingredient in virtually all sunless tanning lotions, sprays and mousses. It is produced commercially by fermentation or chemical conversion of glycerol.\n\nIn looksmaxxing it is the standard way to add a tan without UV exposure. It gives a temporary golden-brown colour that fades over several days as the outer skin sheds. It provides little or no sun protection, so it does not replace sunscreen.","mechanism":"DHA reacts with free amino groups of proteins and amino acids in the stratum corneum through the Maillard reaction, forming brown pigments called melanoidins. The colour is therefore a surface stain rather than a change in melanin production, and it fades as the keratinised cells exfoliate.","risks":"Topical use is considered low risk and DHA is a permitted colour additive for external application in the US. Reviews note it should not be ingested or inhaled, so spray-booth use needs eye, mouth and nose protection, and that long-term and inhalation safety data are limited. Skin irritation or contact allergy is uncommon. A bigger practical risk is false confidence about sun protection, since the tan does not shield against UV.","scholarly":[{"title":"Properties and safety of topical dihydroxyacetone in sunless tanning products: A review","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37697919/","source":"Photodermatology, Photoimmunology & Photomedicine","year":2023},{"title":"Dihydroxyacetone: A Review","url":"https://jddonline.com/articles/dermatology/S1545961618P0387X","source":"Journal of Drugs in Dermatology","year":2018}],"forums":[],"image":null,"imageCredit":null},"Canthaxanthin":{"grokipedia":"https://grokipedia.com/page/Canthaxanthin","overview":"Canthaxanthin is an orange-red carotenoid found in some bacteria, algae, fungi and animals such as flamingos and certain fish. It is used as a colour additive in animal feed, for example to tint salmon and egg yolks.\n\nIn the 1980s it was sold in high-dose oral \"tanning pills\" that deposited pigment in the skin to give an orange-brown tint. This was not a real tan and offered no reliable UV protection, and the products were later restricted or warned against by regulators in several countries.","mechanism":"When taken at high doses over time the fat-soluble pigment accumulates in subcutaneous fat and skin, giving a diffuse orange coloration, a process distinct from melanin production. The same lipid-soluble pigment can also collect in other tissues, including the retina, where it forms the crystalline deposits seen in users.","risks":"High cumulative intake of tanning-pill doses has been associated with canthaxanthin crystalline retinopathy, in which glittering yellow crystals appear around the macula; visual function is often preserved but deposits can persist for years. Other reported problems include gastrointestinal upset, hives and rare liver or blood effects. In the US the FDA has not approved it for tanning, so use for that purpose is unapproved and not recommended.","scholarly":[{"title":"The Extent of Retinal Involvement of Canthaxanthin Crystalline Retinopathy Demonstrated by Multimodal Imaging","url":"https://mdanderson.elsevierpure.com/en/publications/the-extent-of-retinal-involvement-of-canthaxanthin-crystalline-re","source":"MD Anderson / Elsevier Pure","year":null}],"forums":[],"image":null,"imageCredit":null},"Beta-carotene":{"grokipedia":null,"overview":"Beta-carotene is a yellow-orange carotenoid pigment found in carrots, sweet potatoes and leafy greens, and it is a dietary precursor of vitamin A. It is sold as a supplement and used in some sunless-tan and skin-glow products.\n\nIn appearance circles it is used to add a warm, golden-yellow tint to the skin. Small human trials suggest that sustained intake raises skin yellowness and that observers can rate the resulting faces as healthier or more attractive, mainly in lightly pigmented skin.","mechanism":"After absorption with dietary fat, beta-carotene circulates in lipoproteins and is deposited in the skin, particularly in the stratum corneum and subcutaneous fat. The deposited pigment adds a yellow-orange hue to the skin's reflectance. Some is converted to retinol in the body, and it also has antioxidant activity in tissues.","risks":"Very high intake can cause carotenemia, a harmless but visible orange discolouration that fades after intake is reduced. More importantly, large randomized trials in smokers and people exposed to asbestos found that high-dose beta-carotene supplements increased lung cancer risk, so it is not advisable for these groups. The cosmetic effect is modest and variable and is weaker in darker, sun-exposed skin.","scholarly":[{"title":"Effect of beta-carotene supplementation on African skin","url":"https://research-portal.st-andrews.ac.uk/en/publications/effect-of-beta-carotene-supplementation-on-african-skin/","source":"University of St Andrews Research Portal","year":2014},{"title":"Beta-carotene accumulation in serum and skin","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/8424385/","source":"PubMed","year":1993}],"forums":[],"image":null,"imageCredit":null},"Lycopene":{"grokipedia":"https://grokipedia.com/page/Lycopene","overview":"Lycopene is the red carotenoid pigment that gives tomatoes, watermelon and pink grapefruit their colour. It is consumed through food, especially cooked tomato products, and as a supplement.\n\nIn skincare it is promoted as an internal photoprotective antioxidant. Intervention trials suggest small reductions in UV-induced redness and some markers of photodamage, but the effects are modest and lycopene does not replace sunscreen.","mechanism":"Lycopene is a highly lipophilic molecule that accumulates in skin and is an efficient quencher of singlet oxygen and other reactive oxygen species. By limiting UV-driven oxidative damage and downstream inflammatory and matrix-degrading signals, it is thought to blunt part of the sunburn response. Absorption improves when it is eaten with fat and after heat processing.","risks":"Dietary and supplement doses are generally well tolerated. Very high intake can cause lycopenemia, a harmless orange tint to the skin that resolves after reducing intake. Many trials are small and often use tomato paste or industry-supplied extracts, so findings should be read cautiously.","scholarly":[{"title":"The effect of tomato and lycopene on clinical characteristics and molecular markers of UV-induced skin deterioration: a systematic review and meta-analysis of intervention trials","url":"https://www.lycored.com/document/the-effect-of-tomato-and-lycopene-on-clinical-characteristics-and-molecular-markers-of-uv-induced-skin-deterioration-a-systematic-review-and-meta-analysis-of-intervention-trials/","source":"Critical Reviews in Food Science and Nutrition (hosted by Lycored, a supplier)","year":2024},{"title":"Carotenoids and protection against solar UV radiation","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12239422/","source":"PubMed","year":2002}],"forums":[],"image":null,"imageCredit":null},"Astaxanthin":{"grokipedia":"https://grokipedia.com/page/Astaxanthin","overview":"Astaxanthin is a red-pink xanthophyll carotenoid made by microalgae such as Haematococcus pluvialis and responsible for the colour of salmon, shrimp and flamingos. It is sold as a dietary supplement, typically derived from algae.\n\nIn looksmaxxing and skincare it is used for skin hydration, elasticity and defence against UV damage. Systematic reviews find modest improvements in moisture and elasticity in small trials, with less consistent effects on wrinkles.","mechanism":"Astaxanthin is a potent lipid-soluble antioxidant that spans cell membranes and quenches reactive oxygen species, and in cell studies it dampens UV-induced inflammatory signalling and collagen-degrading enzymes. Whether these effects translate into meaningful clinical change depends on the trials, which are mostly short.","risks":"Supplements are generally well tolerated at studied doses, with occasional mild gastrointestinal effects and, at high intake, a reddish skin tint. Safety in pregnancy and long-term use is not well established. Most trials are small, many are industry-funded, and several enrolled mainly healthy Japanese women, limiting how far results generalise.","scholarly":[{"title":"Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis on the Effects of Astaxanthin on Human Skin Ageing","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8472736","source":"Nutrients","year":2021},{"title":"Effects of Astaxanthin Supplementation on Skin Health: A Systematic Review of Clinical Studies","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32202443/","source":"Journal of Dietary Supplements","year":2021}],"forums":[],"image":null,"imageCredit":null},"Polypodium leucotomos":{"grokipedia":null,"overview":"Polypodium leucotomos is a tropical fern native to Central and South America whose leaf extract is sold as an oral supplement, for example under the Fernblock and Heliocare names. It has a long history in traditional medicine and has been studied in dermatology for UV-related skin reactions.\n\nIn the appearance space it is used as an \"internal sunscreen\" adjunct to reduce sunburn, photoaging and pigmentation. Clinical studies suggest modest benefit, but it is not a substitute for sunscreen.","mechanism":"The extract is rich in phenolic compounds with antioxidant and anti-inflammatory activity. In studies it reduces UV-induced DNA damage and inflammatory mediators, limits generation of reactive oxygen species, and may support Langerhans cell function in skin. It does not absorb UV like a sunscreen filter.","risks":"Oral extract is generally well tolerated, with occasional mild gastrointestinal upset, headache or itching; serious adverse effects have not been reported in published reviews. Evidence comes from small trials with varied formulations, so supplements that do not specify the studied extract may not be equivalent.","scholarly":[{"title":"Clinical Efficacy & Safety of Oral Polypodium Leucotomos Extract for Photoprotection: A Systematic Review","url":"https://jofskin.org/33014/index.php/skin/article/view/413","source":"SKIN: The Journal of Cutaneous Medicine","year":2019},{"title":"The Utility of Oral Polypodium Leucotomos Extract for Dermatologic Diseases: A Systematic Review","url":"https://jddonline.com/articles/dermatology/S1545961625P8410X","source":"Journal of Drugs in Dermatology","year":2025},{"title":"Polypodium leucotomos extract: a nutraceutical with photoprotective properties","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/17728848/","source":"PubMed","year":null}],"forums":[],"image":null,"imageCredit":null},"Glutathione":{"grokipedia":"https://grokipedia.com/page/Glutathione","overview":"Glutathione is a tripeptide made of glutamate, cysteine and glycine and is the main small-molecule antioxidant inside most cells. It is available as oral, sublingual, topical and intravenous products.\n\nIt is heavily marketed, particularly in parts of Asia, for skin lightening, and it is discussed in looksmaxxing forums for the same purpose. Controlled evidence of visible whitening is limited and inconsistent, and professional bodies have warned against intravenous use for this purpose.","mechanism":"Proposed lightening mechanisms include inhibition of tyrosinase, the key melanin-producing enzyme, and a shift from darker eumelanin toward lighter pheomelanin through cysteine availability. Oral glutathione is poorly absorbed and largely broken down in the gut, so it is unclear how much reaches skin melanocytes.","risks":"Intravenous use outside medical indications has been linked to rashes and severe allergic or skin reactions, and some regulators, including the Philippine FDA and dermatology societies, have issued warnings. Injectable products from informal sources carry contamination and dosing risks. Any lightening seen in small studies tends to be mild and may reverse after stopping.","scholarly":[],"forums":[{"title":"The Secret to Lighter Skin with Glutathione","url":"https://looksmax.org/threads/the-secret-to-lighter-skin-with-glutathione.1037899","site":"looksmax.org"},{"title":"Injectable Glutathione: The Real Deal for Skin Lightening","url":"https://looksmax.org/threads/injectable-glutathione-the-real-deal-for-skin-lightening.1984341/","site":"looksmax.org"},{"title":"Skin Whitening with L-Glutathione: Safety and Effectiveness Explained","url":"https://www.physicsforums.com/threads/skin-whitening-with-l-glutathione-safety-and-effectiveness-explained.218508/","site":"Physics Forums"}],"image":null,"imageCredit":null},"L-Cysteine":{"grokipedia":"https://grokipedia.com/page/Cysteine","overview":"L-cysteine is a sulfur-containing amino acid that the body can make from methionine and that is also supplied by protein-rich foods. It is sold as a supplement and used in food processing, and its acetylated form N-acetylcysteine is a well-known medicine.\n\nIn skin-tone discussions it is mentioned as a glutathione precursor and because cysteine participates in melanin pathways. Evidence that it lightens skin is weak and mostly indirect.","mechanism":"Cysteine is the rate-limiting amino acid for glutathione synthesis. Its availability is also thought to steer melanocytes toward pheomelanin, the lighter pigment, rather than eumelanin. Whether supplemental doses meaningfully change visible pigmentation in people is unproven.","risks":"Typical dietary and low-dose supplemental intake is generally tolerated, with possible gastrointestinal upset. High doses can cause nausea and other effects, and people with cystinuria or on certain medications should avoid it without advice. Products marketed for lightening are not approved for that use.","scholarly":[],"forums":[],"image":null,"imageCredit":null},"Teriparatide":{"grokipedia":"https://grokipedia.com/page/Teriparatide","overview":"Teriparatide is a recombinant fragment of human parathyroid hormone (residues 1-34) used as a daily injection to treat severe osteoporosis. Unlike most osteoporosis drugs, which slow bone loss, it stimulates new bone formation.\n\nIt appears in looksmaxxing talk because of speculation that bone-building drugs could enhance jaw or facial bone. There is no evidence for cosmetic skeletal change in healthy adults, and its approval covers osteoporosis only.","mechanism":"Intermittent exposure to PTH fragments stimulates osteoblasts through the PTH1 receptor, increasing bone formation more than resorption in the short term. Continuous exposure instead promotes bone breakdown, which is why the treatment pattern matters. Effects are largest in trabecular bone such as the spine.","risks":"Possible effects include nausea, dizziness, leg cramps and raised blood calcium. Osteosarcoma was seen in high-dose rodent studies, which led to a former boxed warning and treatment duration limits. It is prescription-only, expensive and not appropriate for people with certain bone conditions or prior radiation. It is not an approved or evidence-based cosmetic treatment.","scholarly":[],"forums":[],"image":null,"imageCredit":null},"Romosozumab":{"grokipedia":"https://grokipedia.com/page/Romosozumab","overview":"Romosozumab (Evenity) is a monoclonal antibody given by monthly injection for up to a year to treat osteoporosis in people at high fracture risk. It was approved in Japan, the United States and Europe around 2019.\n\nIt is sometimes mentioned in looksmaxxing contexts as a bone-builder, but its approval covers osteoporosis only, and there is no evidence that it changes facial structure in healthy people.","mechanism":"Romosozumab binds sclerostin, a protein produced by osteocytes that inhibits Wnt signalling in bone-forming cells. Blocking it increases bone formation and reduces bone resorption, giving a dual effect that raises bone density rapidly, especially in the spine and hip.","risks":"It carries a boxed warning for heart attack, stroke and cardiovascular death, and is not given to people with a recent event of this kind. Other concerns include low blood calcium, injection-site reactions, and rare osteonecrosis of the jaw and atypical femur fractures. It is a prescription drug requiring clinical supervision and is not a cosmetic treatment.","scholarly":[],"forums":[],"image":null,"imageCredit":null},"Vitamin K2":{"grokipedia":"https://grokipedia.com/page/Vitamin_K2","overview":"Vitamin K2 is a group of fat-soluble compounds called menaquinones, most commonly sold as MK-4 and MK-7, found in fermented foods such as natto, some cheeses and animal products. It is popular in bone and \"facial structure\" supplement stacks alongside vitamin D and calcium.\n\nIts role is to activate calcium-binding proteins including osteocalcin. Trial results on bone density and fractures are mixed, with the strongest signals coming from high-dose MK-4 studies in Japan, and no evidence that it alters facial bone in healthy adults.","mechanism":"Vitamin K2 is a cofactor for gamma-glutamyl carboxylase, which modifies osteocalcin and matrix Gla protein so they can bind calcium. Carboxylated osteocalcin helps incorporate calcium into bone matrix, and matrix Gla protein is thought to limit calcification of soft tissue such as arteries. MK-7 persists in circulation longer than MK-4.","risks":"Supplements are generally well tolerated, and no upper limit has been set. It can interfere with warfarin and other vitamin K antagonists, so people on anticoagulants should seek medical advice. Claims about cardiovascular or bone benefits are stronger in marketing than in controlled trials.","scholarly":[],"forums":[{"title":"K2 supplements mk4 or mk7 or combination?","url":"https://connect.mayoclinic.org/comment/1497991","site":"Mayo Clinic Connect"},{"title":"Vitamin K: High Doses of MK-4 and MK-7 Show Promising Results","url":"https://connect.mayoclinic.org/comment/1548328/","site":"Mayo Clinic Connect"}],"image":null,"imageCredit":null},"Calcium":{"grokipedia":"https://grokipedia.com/page/Calcium","overview":"Calcium is the most abundant mineral in the human body, with the great majority stored in bones and teeth, where it provides rigidity. It is obtained from dairy, fortified foods, leafy greens and supplements.\n\nAdequate intake supports bone mineralisation and peak bone mass, which matters for the skeleton overall. Supplementation helps mainly when dietary intake is low; extra calcium does not reshape facial bone in people who already get enough.","mechanism":"Calcium is deposited as hydroxyapatite in the bone matrix, while a small circulating fraction signals muscle contraction, nerve transmission and clotting. Blood levels are tightly regulated by parathyroid hormone, vitamin D and calcitonin, with bone acting as a reservoir that is drawn on when intake falls short.","risks":"Excess intake from supplements can cause constipation, kidney stones and hypercalcaemia, and some studies have raised questions about cardiovascular effects of high-dose supplements. Dietary sources are preferred where possible, and calcium can interact with certain antibiotics and thyroid medicines.","scholarly":[],"forums":[],"image":null,"imageCredit":null},"Magnesium":{"grokipedia":"https://grokipedia.com/page/Magnesium","overview":"Magnesium is an essential mineral involved in hundreds of enzyme reactions, including energy metabolism, muscle and nerve function and bone structure. About half of the body's magnesium is held in bone.\n\nIt is a common supplement for sleep, cramps and general bone health. The strongest evidence is for correcting a deficiency; beyond that, claims about cosmetic or structural benefit are weak.","mechanism":"Magnesium acts as a cofactor for ATP-dependent enzymes and influences calcium handling, parathyroid hormone secretion and vitamin D activation, all of which affect bone turnover. It is also incorporated into the bone mineral surface and acts as a reservoir.","risks":"Supplements, especially magnesium oxide and citrate, can cause diarrhoea and abdominal cramping, and people with kidney impairment can accumulate it to dangerous levels. It can interact with some antibiotics and bisphosphonates. Dietary intake from nuts, seeds, legumes and greens is generally safe.","scholarly":[],"forums":[],"image":null,"imageCredit":null},"Boron":{"grokipedia":"https://grokipedia.com/page/Boron","overview":"Boron is a trace element found in fruit, vegetables, nuts and legumes and is sold as a supplement, typically as boric acid, calcium fructoborate or boron citrate. It is not classed as an essential nutrient in humans, although it is considered beneficial.\n\nIn bone-focused circles it is used for its possible effects on mineral and sex-hormone metabolism. Human evidence is limited to small studies, and it should not be taken to enhance facial bone structure.","mechanism":"Proposed effects include influencing the metabolism of calcium, magnesium and vitamin D, and modulating steroid hormone levels and inflammatory markers. The exact mechanisms in humans are not well established.","risks":"Normal food intake is safe, but high supplemental doses can cause nausea, diarrhoea and other toxicity, and an upper intake level has been set by authorities. Concerns include reproductive and developmental effects at high exposures, so it should be avoided in pregnancy without advice.","scholarly":[],"forums":[],"image":null,"imageCredit":null},"Caffeine (topical)":{"grokipedia":"https://grokipedia.com/page/Caffeine","overview":"Topical caffeine is included in many under-eye creams and serums intended to reduce puffiness and dark circles. It is the same xanthine alkaloid found in coffee and tea, used here at low concentrations in a cosmetic base.\n\nThe effect is cosmetic and temporary. Some small studies show reduced swelling and improved appearance, but the evidence is limited and often uses short follow-up and company-run designs.","mechanism":"Caffeine is a vasoconstrictor that narrows small vessels, which may reduce visible blood pooling and fluid in the thin periorbital skin. It may also act as an antioxidant and may promote lipolysis and drainage in tissue, although penetration through skin is modest and effects are short-lived.","risks":"Topical use is usually well tolerated, with occasional irritation or allergy. Contact with the eyes should be avoided. Absorbed amounts are small, but product claims are often stronger than the evidence, and the effect does not address structural causes of eye bags such as fat prolapse.","scholarly":[],"forums":[],"image":null,"imageCredit":null},"Hesperidin":{"grokipedia":"https://grokipedia.com/page/Hesperidin","overview":"Hesperidin is a flavanone glycoside concentrated in the peel of citrus fruits. It is sold as a supplement, often combined with diosmin in products used for chronic venous insufficiency and haemorrhoids.\n\nIn the context of facial puffiness and dark circles it is occasionally added to creams and supplements for its effects on small blood vessels. Evidence for periorbital use specifically is limited, and most clinical data concern venous disorders.","mechanism":"Hesperidin and its aglycone hesperetin are thought to improve vascular tone and capillary resistance, reduce microvascular permeability and dampen inflammation through antioxidant pathways. Oral bioavailability is low and depends on gut bacteria releasing the active aglycone.","risks":"Generally well tolerated, with mild gastrointestinal upset, headache or rash occasionally reported. It is recognised as safe as a food ingredient, but interactions with some medications that are metabolised by the liver are possible, and evidence for cosmetic claims is weak.","scholarly":[],"forums":[],"image":null,"imageCredit":null},"Dandelion extract":{"grokipedia":"https://grokipedia.com/page/Taraxacum","overview":"Dandelion extract comes from the leaves or roots of Taraxacum officinale and is traditionally used as a mild herbal diuretic and digestive aid. It is sold as tea, capsules and tinctures.\n\nIn the appearance space it is marketed for \"water weight\" and facial bloating. Human evidence for a meaningful diuretic effect is very limited, so the claimed benefit for face depuffing is largely unsupported.","mechanism":"Dandelion leaf contains potassium and compounds that may promote modest urine output in animal studies, and the root contains inulin and sesquiterpene lactones. A small pilot study reported a short-lived increase in urination, but this is not strong evidence of a clinically significant effect.","risks":"Allergic reactions can occur in people sensitive to the daisy family (Asteraceae), including ragweed. It may interact with diuretics, lithium, anticoagulants and some antibiotics, and could raise potassium in susceptible people. Quality of herbal products varies.","scholarly":[],"forums":[],"image":null,"imageCredit":null},"Potassium":{"grokipedia":"https://grokipedia.com/page/Potassium","overview":"Potassium is an essential electrolyte that governs fluid balance, nerve signalling and muscle contraction, and is abundant in fruit, vegetables, legumes and dairy. Most people get it from food rather than supplements.\n\nIn depuffing discussions it is mentioned because a higher potassium-to-sodium balance supports normal fluid regulation. Fixing a high-sodium diet is more relevant than taking pills, and evidence for facial de-bloating from potassium supplements is thin.","mechanism":"Potassium is the main intracellular cation. It works with sodium and the kidney to set extracellular fluid volume, and higher potassium intake promotes sodium excretion and lowers blood pressure. The face may look less puffy when overall fluid retention falls, though many causes of facial puffiness are unrelated to electrolytes.","risks":"Over-the-counter potassium supplements are limited in dose because excess can cause dangerous hyperkalaemia and heart rhythm problems, particularly in people with kidney disease or taking ACE inhibitors, ARBs or potassium-sparing diuretics. Cosmetic use should not override medical advice.","scholarly":[],"forums":[],"image":null,"imageCredit":null},"Furosemide":{"grokipedia":"https://grokipedia.com/page/Furosemide","overview":"Furosemide (Lasix) is a potent loop diuretic used to treat fluid overload from heart failure, kidney disease and cirrhosis, and sometimes high blood pressure. It is prescription-only in nearly all countries.\n\nIt turns up in appearance and bodybuilding circles as a way to drop water weight quickly. For face slimming it is both dangerous and ineffective, since any change reflects temporary fluid loss, not fat or bone.","mechanism":"Furosemide blocks the sodium-potassium-chloride cotransporter in the thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle, so the kidney excretes more sodium, chloride, potassium and water. Onset is quick and the effect is strong, which makes electrolyte disturbances easy to cause.","risks":"Misuse can cause dehydration, low potassium, sodium and magnesium, cramps, dizziness, kidney injury, hearing damage and dangerous heart rhythms. It can interact with many drugs and is contraindicated in anuria and sulfonamide hypersensitivity. Use without medical supervision for cosmetic purposes is unsafe and is prohibited in many sports.","scholarly":[],"forums":[],"image":null,"imageCredit":null},"Creatine monohydrate":{"grokipedia":"https://grokipedia.com/page/Creatine","overview":"Creatine monohydrate is the most widely studied sports supplement. Creatine itself is a nitrogen-containing compound made in the body from amino acids and also obtained from meat and fish; the monohydrate salt is the standard supplemental form. It is sold as a cheap, flavourless powder.\n\nIn physique and performance circles it is used to support strength, lean mass gain during resistance training and repeated high-intensity effort. Evidence from many randomized trials and position stands supports modest but reliable gains in these outcomes, which is why it is rated strong here.","mechanism":"Muscle stores creatine mostly as phosphocreatine, which rapidly regenerates ATP during short, intense efforts. Supplementation raises intramuscular creatine content, which can allow slightly more training work to be done and may encourage greater adaptation over time. Part of the early scale-weight increase reflects water drawn into muscle cells.","risks":"Creatine is legal and over the counter. Reviews of long-term use in healthy people have found it generally well tolerated, with water retention and occasional gastrointestinal upset the most common complaints. It can modestly raise serum creatinine without necessarily reflecting kidney damage, which can confuse lab interpretation, and people with kidney disease should seek medical advice first. Responses vary and some people are non-responders.","scholarly":[{"title":"International Society of Sports Nutrition position stand: safety and efficacy of creatine supplementation in exercise, sport, and medicine","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5469049/","source":"Journal of the International Society of Sports Nutrition","year":2017},{"title":"Common questions and misconceptions about creatine supplementation: what does the scientific evidence really show?","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7871530/","source":"Journal of the International Society of Sports Nutrition","year":2021}],"forums":[],"image":null,"imageCredit":null},"Whey protein":{"grokipedia":"https://grokipedia.com/page/Whey_protein","overview":"Whey protein is the liquid fraction of milk left over from cheese making, dried into concentrate, isolate or hydrolysate powders. It is a complete protein that digests quickly and is rich in leucine and other essential amino acids.\n\nLifters and dieters use it as a convenient way to reach daily protein targets, preserve lean mass while cutting and support muscle gain alongside resistance training. Meta-analyses show a small to moderate benefit for strength and lean mass, larger in older adults and in people who otherwise eat little protein.","mechanism":"Whey supplies a rapid rise in blood amino acids, particularly leucine, which stimulates muscle protein synthesis through mTORC1 signalling. The benefit comes mainly from adding to total daily protein, so the advantage over other complete protein sources is small when overall intake is already adequate.","risks":"Whey is a food product and legal everywhere. Lactose intolerance or milk allergy can cause digestive symptoms or reactions, with isolates containing less lactose. Quality control varies by brand, with occasional heavy-metal or labelling concerns. Very high intakes offer no added benefit, and people with kidney disease should follow medical guidance.","scholarly":[{"title":"The Effect of Whey Protein Supplementation on the Temporal Recovery of Muscle Function Following Resistance Training: A Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC5852797/","source":"Nutrients / PMC","year":2018},{"title":"Effect of whey protein supplementation during resistance training sessions on body mass and muscular strength: a meta-analysis","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31041966/","source":"PubMed","year":2019},{"title":"Comparative Meta-Analysis of the Effect of Concentrated, Hydrolyzed, and Isolated Whey Protein Supplementation on Body Composition of Physical Activity Practitioners","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC6769754/","source":"PubMed Central","year":2019}],"forums":[],"image":null,"imageCredit":null},"Leucine":{"grokipedia":"https://grokipedia.com/page/Leucine","overview":"Leucine is an essential branched-chain amino acid found in all dietary proteins, with especially high amounts in dairy, meat, eggs and soy. It is also sold as a standalone powder or as part of BCAA and essential amino acid products.\n\nIn the muscle-building context it is used to push muscle protein synthesis, particularly in older adults or in people eating little protein. The strong rating reflects solid mechanistic and acute-response data, though extra isolated leucine adds little for people who already eat enough complete protein.","mechanism":"Leucine acts as a signal of amino acid availability. Sensors such as Sestrin2 respond to intracellular leucine and release inhibition of the mTORC1 complex, which promotes translation initiation and muscle protein synthesis. Insulin release triggered by leucine contributes to the same pathway.","risks":"Leucine is a normal dietary component and legal. High supplemental doses can cause digestive upset and may disturb the balance of other branched-chain amino acids, and it is dangerous in maple syrup urine disease. Long-term muscle-growth benefits from isolated leucine in healthy, well-fed trainees are not well demonstrated.","scholarly":[{"title":"Nutritional and contractile regulation of human skeletal muscle protein synthesis and mTORC1 signaling","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2698645/","source":"Journal of Applied Physiology","year":2009},{"title":"Kinetic modeling of leucine-mediated signaling and protein metabolism in human skeletal muscle","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC10767222/","source":"PubMed Central","year":2023},{"title":"Leucine elicits myotube hypertrophy and enhances maximal contractile force in tissue engineered skeletal muscle in vitro","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC5518187/","source":"PubMed Central","year":2017}],"forums":[],"image":null,"imageCredit":null},"Beta-alanine":{"grokipedia":"https://grokipedia.com/page/%CE%92-Alanine","overview":"Beta-alanine is a non-proteinogenic amino acid and the rate-limiting building block of carnosine, a dipeptide stored in skeletal muscle. It is sold as a powder or capsule, often in pre-workout blends.\n\nAthletes use it to improve performance in efforts lasting roughly one to four minutes and to get more work done in high-repetition sets. The effect is modest and does not directly add muscle, but the evidence base supports it as an ergogenic aid, hence the strong rating.","mechanism":"Taking beta-alanine for several weeks raises muscle carnosine content. Carnosine helps buffer hydrogen ions that accumulate during intense exercise, delaying fatigue. The familiar tingling (paraesthesia) comes from activation of sensory nerve receptors and is not a sign of effect.","risks":"Paraesthesia is the main side effect and is harmless, though uncomfortable to some; divided or slow-release forms reduce it. Beta-alanine is legal and not banned in sport. Long-term data in healthy adults show no clear toxicity at studied intakes. It does little for single-effort maximal strength, so expectations should be limited to high-rep and endurance-type work.","scholarly":[{"title":"International society of sports nutrition position stand: Beta-Alanine","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4501114/","source":"Journal of the International Society of Sports Nutrition","year":2015},{"title":"Beta-Alanine Supplementation Improved 10-km Running Time Trial in Physically Active Adults","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6092497/","source":"PubMed Central","year":2018}],"forums":[],"image":null,"imageCredit":null},"HMB":{"grokipedia":null,"overview":"HMB (beta-hydroxy-beta-methylbutyrate) is a metabolite of leucine, sold as a calcium salt or free acid. It originated in studies of reducing muscle breakdown and has been used by lifters during hard training or calorie restriction.\n\nResults depend heavily on who is studied. Meta-analyses suggest it helps preserve muscle in older adults and in clinical settings, while trials in trained young athletes mostly show little effect, consistent with a moderate rating.","mechanism":"HMB is thought to reduce protein breakdown through effects on the ubiquitin-proteasome pathway and to modestly support protein synthesis signalling and muscle cell repair. Human data for these mechanisms are less consistent than animal and cell work.","risks":"HMB is legal and has a good short-term safety record in trials. Reported side effects are minor, mostly gastrointestinal. Benefits in experienced trainees eating enough protein and training progressively are unconvincing, and quality of products varies.","scholarly":[{"title":"International society of sports nutrition position stand: beta-hydroxy-beta-methylbutyrate (HMB)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC11740297/","source":"Journal of the International Society of Sports Nutrition","year":2025},{"title":"Effects of beta-hydroxy-beta-methylbutyrate supplementation on strength and body composition in trained and competitive athletes: A meta-analysis of randomized controlled trials","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29249685/","source":"PubMed","year":2018},{"title":"Effect of beta-hydroxy-beta-methylbutyrate supplementation on muscle loss in older adults: a systematic review and meta-analysis","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26169182/","source":"PubMed","year":2015}],"forums":[],"image":null,"imageCredit":null},"Citrulline malate":{"grokipedia":"https://grokipedia.com/page/Citrulline","overview":"Citrulline malate combines the amino acid L-citrulline with malic acid. Citrulline is an intermediate of the urea cycle and a precursor of arginine, and is a staple of pre-workout powders.\n\nIt is used for muscle pump, endurance in resistance sets and reduced soreness. Meta-analyses find a small improvement in repetitions to failure with low certainty, and single trials often show no effect.","mechanism":"Citrulline is converted to arginine in the kidney, which raises nitric oxide production and blood vessel dilation. It may also help clear ammonia and lactate-related fatigue, and malate may feed energy metabolism, though these steps are not firmly proven for performance.","risks":"Citrulline malate is legal and generally well tolerated; the main complaint is stomach upset at larger amounts. It can lower blood pressure slightly, relevant for people on antihypertensive or erectile dysfunction drugs. Benefits for muscle size are not established.","scholarly":[{"title":"A critical review of citrulline malate supplementation and exercise performance","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8571142/","source":"PubMed Central","year":2021},{"title":"Effects of Citrulline Malate Supplementation on Exercise Performance: A Systematic Review and Three-Level Meta-Analysis","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13304508/","source":"PubMed Central","year":null},{"title":"Acute Effect of L-Citrulline Supplementation on Resistance Exercise Performance and Muscle Oxygenation in Recreationally Resistance Trained Men and Women","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10366749/","source":"PubMed Central","year":2023}],"forums":[],"image":null,"imageCredit":null},"Betaine":{"grokipedia":"https://grokipedia.com/page/Betaine","overview":"Betaine, also called trimethylglycine, is a small compound found in beets, spinach, wheat and shellfish. It is also produced in the body from choline and sold as a supplement for sports use.\n\nStudies have looked at strength, power and body composition. A meta-analysis suggests a small gain in maximal strength, mainly lower body, while evidence for body composition and power is inconsistent.","mechanism":"Betaine acts as an osmolyte that protects cells under stress and as a methyl donor, converting homocysteine to methionine. Proposed performance effects include better cell hydration, creatine synthesis support and effects on anabolic signalling, though human data are limited.","risks":"Betaine is legal and has low acute toxicity. Gastrointestinal upset and a fishy odor can occur, and it may raise cholesterol in some people. It is a prescription medicine for homocystinuria; betaine hydrochloride sold for digestion is a different product. Trials are small and short.","scholarly":[{"title":"Ergogenic effects of betaine supplementation on strength and power performance","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2915951/","source":"Journal of the International Society of Sports Nutrition","year":2010},{"title":"Effects of chronic betaine supplementation on exercise performance: Systematic review and meta-analysis","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39514262/","source":"PubMed","year":2024},{"title":"Betaine Supplementation Does not Improve Muscle Hypertrophy or Strength Following 6 Weeks of Cross-Fit Training","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7352895/","source":"PubMed Central","year":2020}],"forums":[],"image":null,"imageCredit":null},"Ashwagandha":{"grokipedia":"https://grokipedia.com/page/Withania_somnifera","overview":"Ashwagandha (Withania somnifera) is a shrub whose root is a staple of Ayurvedic medicine. Extracts are marketed as adaptogens for stress, sleep and vitality.\n\nIn fitness use it is taken to support strength, recovery and testosterone. Small randomized trials report modest gains in strength and some hormone readings, but samples are small and funding often industry-linked, hence a moderate rating.","mechanism":"The active constituents are withanolides, which appear to modulate the stress axis, lower cortisol and affect GABAergic and inflammatory signalling. Any effect on muscle likely comes indirectly through improved stress, sleep and recovery rather than direct anabolism.","risks":"Ashwagandha is sold as a supplement in most countries. Rare cases of liver injury have been reported, and it can affect thyroid hormone levels and cause drowsiness or stomach upset. It is avoided in pregnancy and may interact with thyroid, sedative and immunosuppressant drugs.","scholarly":[{"title":"Examining the effect of Withania somnifera supplementation on muscle strength and recovery: a randomized controlled trial","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4658772/","source":"Journal of the International Society of Sports Nutrition","year":2015},{"title":"Effects of Ashwagandha (Withania somnifera) standardized root extract on physical endurance and VO2max in healthy adults performing resistance training","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC11234080/","source":"PubMed Central","year":2024},{"title":"Effects of Withania somnifera (Ashwagandha) Supplementation on Exercise Performance: A Systematic Review and Three-Level Meta-Analysis","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13304793/","source":"PubMed Central","year":null}],"forums":[],"image":null,"imageCredit":null},"Turkesterone":{"grokipedia":"https://grokipedia.com/page/Turkesterone","overview":"Turkesterone is a phytoecdysteroid, a plant steroid structurally related to insect molting hormones, first isolated from Ajuga turkestanica. It became popular in supplements as a supposed natural anabolic.\n\nClaims rest largely on rodent studies and on research into the better-known ecdysterone. Human trials of turkesterone specifically are small and mostly do not show meaningful muscle or strength benefits, so the rating is limited.","mechanism":"In animals, ecdysteroids appear to increase muscle protein synthesis, possibly via estrogen receptor beta and signalling pathways, rather than through the androgen receptor. How well turkesterone is absorbed and whether this translates to people are unresolved.","risks":"It is sold as a dietary supplement in the US, with unresolved novel-food status in the EU. Short-term human studies have not reported serious harm, but long-term data are lacking and independent testing has found products with far less turkesterone than labelled.","scholarly":[{"title":"Ecdysterone and Turkesterone: Compounds with Prominent Potential in Sport and Healthy Nutrition","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11085066/","source":"PubMed Central","year":null},{"title":"Phytoecdysteroids Do Not Have Anabolic Effects in Skeletal Muscle...","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7825148/","source":"PubMed Central","year":null},{"title":"The effects of an ecdysteroid supplement on indices of physical and mental performance","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12379694/","source":"PubMed Central","year":null}],"forums":[],"image":null,"imageCredit":null},"Ecdysterone":{"grokipedia":"https://grokipedia.com/page/20-Hydroxyecdysone","overview":"Ecdysterone, or 20-hydroxyecdysone, is the main molting hormone of insects and crustaceans and is also found in plants such as spinach and Cyanotis. It is sold as a supplement and is being developed as an investigational drug for some muscle conditions.\n\nA widely cited study in young men reported greater muscle gains with high-dose ecdysterone alongside training, but replication is lacking and other findings are mixed, so the evidence remains preliminary.","mechanism":"In rodents ecdysterone stimulates muscle protein synthesis without androgen receptor activity, with evidence pointing to estrogen receptor beta and downstream Akt signalling. Human mechanism data are thin.","risks":"Acute toxicity appears low. It is sold as a supplement but the World Anti-Doping Agency has monitored it, and product purity is variable. Long-term human safety is not established.","scholarly":[{"title":"20-Hydroxyecdysone, from Plant Extracts to Clinical Use","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8146789/","source":"PubMed Central","year":2021},{"title":"Phytoecdysteroids: Distribution, Structural Diversity, Biosynthesis...","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9369314/","source":"PubMed Central","year":null},{"title":"Ecdysterone and Turkesterone: Compounds with Prominent Potential in Sport and Healthy Nutrition","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11085066/","source":"PubMed Central","year":null}],"forums":[],"image":null,"imageCredit":null},"Testosterone":{"grokipedia":"https://grokipedia.com/page/Testosterone","overview":"Testosterone is the principal male sex hormone, an androgen produced mainly in the testes. It drives male secondary sexual characteristics, bone density, red blood cell production and muscle mass.\n\nIn medicine it is prescribed to treat diagnosed hypogonadism. Healthy young men given supraphysiologic doses gain muscle in a dose-dependent way, which is why anabolic steroid use is a feature of physique culture. Benefit is strong where there is true deficiency; use beyond that carries serious risks.","mechanism":"Testosterone binds the androgen receptor, increasing muscle protein synthesis, satellite cell activity and bone formation, and is partly converted to estradiol and dihydrotestosterone with their own effects.","risks":"It is a prescription-only, controlled substance in many countries. Risks include suppression of natural production and fertility, acne, polycythemia, blood pressure and lipid changes, mood effects and possible cardiovascular strain; abrupt cessation can leave a deficiency. This entry is informational and not guidance for use.","scholarly":[{"title":"Testosterone dose-response relationships in healthy young men (Bhasin et al.)","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11701431/","source":"American Journal of Physiology - Endocrinology and Metabolism","year":2001}],"forums":[],"image":null,"imageCredit":null},"Somatropin":{"grokipedia":"https://grokipedia.com/page/Somatropin","overview":"Somatropin is recombinant human growth hormone, identical in sequence to the pituitary hormone. It is a prescription medicine for children and adults with growth hormone deficiency and several other conditions.\n\nIt is also used illicitly for body composition and anti-aging. Evidence is strong in deficiency; in healthy people it can add some lean mass but with limited strength benefit and notable side effects.","mechanism":"It binds growth hormone receptors and activates JAK2/STAT signalling, which prompts the liver and other tissues to produce IGF-1. The result is growth, fat mobilization and effects on glucose metabolism and fluid balance.","risks":"Somatropin is prescription-only and banned in sport. Side effects include fluid retention, joint pain, carpal tunnel syndrome and insulin resistance. Chronic excess causes acromegaly, with enlargement of the jaw, brow and hands, plus heart and metabolic problems and a possible cancer link. This is informational, not guidance for use.","scholarly":[{"title":"History of growth hormone therapy","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3183530/","source":"Indian Journal of Endocrinology and Metabolism","year":null},{"title":"Adult growth hormone deficiency","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3183535/","source":"PubMed Central","year":null}],"forums":[],"image":null,"imageCredit":null},"MK-677":{"grokipedia":"https://grokipedia.com/page/Ibutamoren","overview":"MK-677 (ibutamoren) is an orally active, non-peptide drug that mimics ghrelin and raises growth hormone and IGF-1. It was developed by Merck but never approved, and is sold as a research chemical.\n\nTrials in older adults and obese men showed increased fat-free mass but no clear functional improvement, and there is no demonstrated cosmetic benefit, matching the unproven rating.","mechanism":"It activates the ghrelin receptor (GHS-R1a) in the pituitary and hypothalamus, producing pulsatile growth hormone release and downstream IGF-1 elevation, plus increased appetite.","risks":"Not approved for any use and prohibited in sport. Reported effects include increased appetite, fluid retention, lethargy and reduced insulin sensitivity; a heart failure signal appeared in one trial. Long-term effects of sustained IGF-1 elevation are unknown and products may be mislabelled.","scholarly":[{"title":"Effects of an oral ghrelin mimetic on body composition and clinical outcomes in healthy older adults (Nass et al.)","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18981485/","source":"Annals of Internal Medicine","year":2008},{"title":"MK-677, an orally active growth hormone secretagogue, reverses diet-induced catabolism...","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9467534/","source":"Journal of Clinical Endocrinology & Metabolism","year":1998},{"title":"The Safety and Efficacy of Growth Hormone Secretagogues","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5632578/","source":"PubMed Central","year":null}],"forums":[],"image":null,"imageCredit":null},"CJC-1295":{"grokipedia":"https://grokipedia.com/page/CJC-1295","overview":"CJC-1295 is a synthetic analogue of growth hormone releasing hormone developed in the early 2000s. The long-acting version binds albumin, extending its half-life.\n\nEarly human studies showed raised growth hormone and IGF-1, but development stopped and no data show a muscle or cosmetic benefit, so it is rated unproven.","mechanism":"It binds the GHRH receptor on pituitary somatotrophs, increasing growth hormone secretion and IGF-1 levels for days after a single administration in the long-acting form.","risks":"It is not an approved medicine, is sold as a research peptide, and is banned in sport. A phase II trial was halted after a death in a participant. Reported effects include flushing, headache, water retention and injection-site reactions. Long-term safety is unknown and the regulatory status of compounded versions has been in flux.","scholarly":[{"title":"Prolonged stimulation of growth hormone (GH) and insulin-like growth factor I secretion by CJC-1295...","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16352683/","source":"Journal of Clinical Endocrinology & Metabolism","year":2006},{"title":"Once-daily administration of CJC-1295, a long-acting growth hormone-releasing hormone analogue...","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16822960/","source":"PubMed","year":2006},{"title":"Activation of the GH/IGF-1 axis by CJC-1295, a long-acting GHRH analog...","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19386527/","source":"PubMed","year":null}],"forums":[],"image":null,"imageCredit":null},"Ipamorelin":{"grokipedia":"https://grokipedia.com/page/Ipamorelin","overview":"Ipamorelin is a synthetic pentapeptide that selectively stimulates growth hormone release. Novo Nordisk developed it in the 1990s, and it is now sold as a research peptide.\n\nClinical work has been limited, mainly early pharmacology and a trial in post-surgical bowel recovery that showed no clear benefit. Claims for muscle and fat loss are unsupported by good human data.","mechanism":"It is a selective agonist of the ghrelin receptor (GHS-R1a), triggering pulsatile pituitary growth hormone release with relatively little effect on cortisol and prolactin compared with older secretagogues.","risks":"It is not approved for human use and is banned in sport. Short-term data are limited; theoretical concerns include insulin resistance, fluid retention and effects of long-term GH/IGF-1 elevation. Product quality is unregulated.","scholarly":[{"title":"Ipamorelin, the first selective growth hormone secretagogue","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9849822/","source":"PubMed","year":1998},{"title":"Prospective, randomized, controlled, proof-of-concept study of the ghrelin mimetic ipamorelin for the management of postoperative ileus...","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25331030/","source":"PubMed","year":null},{"title":"Ipamorelin, a new growth-hormone-releasing peptide, induces longitudinal bone growth in rats","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10373343/","source":"PubMed","year":null}],"forums":[],"image":null,"imageCredit":null},"Sermorelin":{"grokipedia":"https://grokipedia.com/page/Sermorelin","overview":"Sermorelin is a synthetic fragment of growth hormone releasing hormone consisting of its first 29 amino acids. It was FDA-approved for diagnosing and treating growth hormone deficiency in children before manufacturing ceased in 2008.\n\nIt is still available through compounding pharmacies and used off-label for age-related decline. The hormonal mechanism is real, but evidence for visible body-composition or cosmetic benefit is thin.","mechanism":"It acts on GHRH receptors in the pituitary to prompt natural, pulsatile growth hormone release, so the body's feedback mechanisms still operate, unlike with injected GH.","risks":"It is prescription-only in the US and banned in competitive sport. Common effects are injection-site reactions, flushing and headache. It is avoided with active cancer and in pregnancy. Quality of compounded preparations and telehealth marketing claims vary.","scholarly":[],"forums":[],"image":null,"imageCredit":null},"GHRP-6":{"grokipedia":"https://grokipedia.com/page/GHRP-6","overview":"GHRP-6 is a synthetic hexapeptide from the first generation of growth hormone releasing peptides. It was a research tool in the 1980s and 1990s and is now sold as a research chemical.\n\nIt is known for raising growth hormone and also for a strong hunger effect. Human evidence of improved muscle or appearance is lacking.","mechanism":"GHRP-6 activates the ghrelin receptor, causing growth hormone release and appetite stimulation, and also raises cortisol and prolactin somewhat.","risks":"It is not approved for human use and is banned in sport. Reported effects include intense hunger, water retention and flushing. Long-term safety is unknown and quality is unregulated.","scholarly":[{"title":"Synthetic Growth Hormone-Releasing Peptides (GHRPs)","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5392015/","source":"PubMed Central","year":null},{"title":"The Safety and Efficacy of Growth Hormone Secretagogues","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5632578/","source":"PubMed Central","year":null},{"title":"Pharmacokinetic study of Growth Hormone-Releasing Peptide 6...","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23099431/","source":"PubMed","year":null}],"forums":[],"image":null,"imageCredit":null},"IGF-1 LR3":{"grokipedia":"https://grokipedia.com/page/IGF-1_LR3","overview":"IGF-1 LR3 is a lab-modified version of insulin-like growth factor 1 with an extended amino acid chain and an arginine substitution. It was developed for cell culture and sold to bodybuilders as an injectable research chemical.\n\nAnimal and cell data show growth effects, but there are no reliable human trials of benefit in healthy people.","mechanism":"It activates the IGF-1 receptor and downstream PI3K/Akt/mTOR signalling. The modifications lower binding to IGF binding proteins, so it stays active longer than native IGF-1.","risks":"Not approved for humans and banned in sport. Main concerns are low blood sugar, fluid retention, joint pain and organ growth, plus theoretical cancer promotion. Products are unregulated.","scholarly":[{"title":"Design and characterisation of long-R3-insulin-like growth factor-I...","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/8919033/","source":"PubMed","year":null},{"title":"Optimizing IGF-I for skeletal muscle therapeutics","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4665094/","source":"PubMed Central","year":null},{"title":"Detection of LongR3-IGF-I, Des(1-3)-IGF-I, and R3-IGF-I using...","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33587816/","source":"PubMed","year":2021}],"forums":[],"image":null,"imageCredit":null},"Follistatin-344":{"grokipedia":null,"overview":"Follistatin-344 refers to a form of the protein follistatin (the 344 label is used by peptide vendors) sold as an experimental myostatin blocker. Follistatin is a natural protein that binds and neutralizes myostatin and activins.\n\nGene therapy work in animals and early clinical trials shows muscle effects, but injected peptide products have no credible human evidence.","mechanism":"Follistatin binds myostatin and activin, preventing them from signalling through their receptors, which relieves the brake on muscle growth in animal models.","risks":"Not approved; banned in sport. Because follistatin affects reproductive hormones and other tissues, blocking these pathways raises theoretical concerns. Marketed peptides are unregulated and may not contain what is claimed.","scholarly":[{"title":"Follistatin induces muscle hypertrophy through satellite cell proliferation and inhibition of both myostatin and activin","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19435857/","source":"PubMed","year":2009},{"title":"Follistatin: a multifunctional regulatory protein","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9799587/","source":"PubMed","year":null},{"title":"Follistatin Mitigates Myofibroblast Differentiation and Collagen Production by Blocking Activin A and Myostatin Action","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6938778/","source":"PubMed Central","year":null}],"forums":[],"image":null,"imageCredit":null},"Ostarine":{"grokipedia":"https://grokipedia.com/page/Enobosarm","overview":"Ostarine (enobosarm, MK-2866) is a nonsteroidal selective androgen receptor modulator developed to treat muscle wasting. It reached phase III trials but was never approved.\n\nTrials showed lean mass gains without matching functional gains. Recreational use is common and a frequent cause of anti-doping violations.","mechanism":"It binds the androgen receptor, aiming for activity in muscle and bone with less effect on tissues such as the prostate. It suppresses natural testosterone production.","risks":"Not approved and banned in sport. Reported harms include hormonal suppression, lipid changes and drug-induced liver injury. Gray-market products are often mislabelled.","scholarly":[{"title":"The selective androgen receptor modulator GTx-024 (enobosarm)...","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3177038/","source":"PubMed Central","year":null},{"title":"Effects of enobosarm on muscle wasting and physical function...","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4898053/","source":"PubMed Central","year":null},{"title":"Drug-Induced Liver Injury From Enobosarm (Ostarine), a Selective Androgen Receptor Modulator","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8337042/","source":"PubMed Central","year":null}],"forums":[],"image":null,"imageCredit":null},"LGD-4033":{"grokipedia":"https://grokipedia.com/page/Ligandrol","overview":"LGD-4033 (ligandrol) is a nonsteroidal selective androgen receptor modulator investigated for muscle wasting. A phase I study found increased lean mass in healthy men, but development stalled.\n\nIt is widely sold as a research chemical. Small trials show lean mass gains, and observations of recreational use show hormone and lipid problems.","mechanism":"It selectively activates the androgen receptor in muscle and bone and suppresses the hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis, lowering natural testosterone.","risks":"Not approved and banned in sport. Documented problems include testosterone suppression, reduced HDL cholesterol and drug-induced liver injury, especially at doses above trial levels. Product contents are unreliable.","scholarly":[{"title":"The Safety, Pharmacokinetics, and Effects of LGD-4033, a Novel Nonsteroidal Oral, Selective Androgen Receptor Modulator, in Healthy Young Men","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4111291/","source":"PubMed Central","year":2013},{"title":"LGD-4033 and a Case of Drug-Induced Liver Injury","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11485217/","source":"PubMed Central","year":2024},{"title":"LGD-4033 and MK-677 use impacts body composition, circulating...","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36303408/","source":"PubMed","year":2022}],"forums":[],"image":null,"imageCredit":null},"RAD-140":{"grokipedia":null,"overview":"RAD-140 (testolone) is a nonsteroidal selective androgen receptor modulator developed by Radius Health, studied in preclinical models for muscle and breast cancer. It has never completed human efficacy trials.\n\nIt is sold as a research chemical for muscle gain, with little human data.","mechanism":"It binds the androgen receptor with tissue selectivity and suppresses natural androgen production.","risks":"Not approved and banned in sport. Case reports link it to drug-induced liver injury, and it suppresses hormones. Insufficient evidence exists that it is safe. Products may be mislabelled.","scholarly":[],"forums":[],"image":null,"imageCredit":null},"YK-11":{"grokipedia":"https://grokipedia.com/page/YK-11","overview":"YK-11 is an experimental steroid-based compound classed as a SARM, though it behaves as a partial androgen receptor agonist. Research is confined to cell studies, mainly from Japanese groups in 2011 to 2013.\n\nIt is sold as a research chemical. No human trials exist.","mechanism":"In cultured muscle cells it increased follistatin expression and reduced myostatin signalling via androgen receptor activation. These results have not been shown in humans.","risks":"Not approved and banned in sport. Safety is untested in humans, and liver, hormonal and cardiovascular effects are suspected. Products sold online are frequently mislabelled.","scholarly":[{"title":"Selective androgen receptor modulator, YK11, regulates myogenic differentiation of C2C12 myoblasts by follistatin expression","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23995658/","source":"Biological and Pharmaceutical Bulletin","year":2013},{"title":"Selective Androgen Receptor Modulator, YK11, Up-Regulates...","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29491216/","source":"PubMed","year":null},{"title":"YK11 induces oxidative stress and mitochondrial dysfunction in...","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37468001/","source":"PubMed","year":null}],"forums":[],"image":null,"imageCredit":null},"Glass nail file + buffer":{"grokipedia":"https://grokipedia.com/page/Nail_file","overview":"A glass (or crystal) nail file is a reusable abrasive tool for shaping the free edge of the fingernails and toenails. Its surface is etched with fine grit, so it removes material slowly and leaves a smoother edge than a paper emery board or a coarse metal file. A buffer is a multi-sided block or strip used afterwards to smooth ridges and add shine to the nail plate.\n\nIn appearance-focused grooming, short, evenly shaped, clean nails are treated as a basic marker of care, since hands are visible in most social interactions. Research directly on how observers rate fingernails is sparse; the cited work looks at the wearer's own psychological state after nail care, so the broader claim that others notice is plausible but only thinly studied. The effect is cosmetic and lasts until the nails grow or chip.","mechanism":"The fine etched grit abrades keratin gradually when drawn in one direction across the nail edge. Fine grit seals the edge more evenly than coarse abrasives, which reduces the splitting and peeling that rough edges encourage. Buffing with progressively finer surfaces levels the surface of the plate so it reflects light more evenly.","risks":"The tool is low-risk. Over-filing or over-buffing thins the nail plate and can leave nails weak or sensitive, and filing wet nails tears them more easily. Shared files can carry fungal or bacterial contamination and should be cleaned. Marketing claims are modest; the main real limitation is that the benefit is cosmetic and temporary.","scholarly":[{"title":"Salon nail care with superficial self-disclosure vitalizes psychological state","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10548882/","source":"Frontiers in Psychology","year":2023}],"forums":[],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/72/Glass_nail_file.jpg","imageCredit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Glass_nail_file.jpg"},"Cuticle oil pen":{"grokipedia":"https://grokipedia.com/page/Cuticle","overview":"A cuticle oil pen is a twist-up applicator containing a blend of plant oils (often jojoba, almond or vitamin E) that is brushed onto the skin around the nail. It is used to soften the proximal nail fold and the narrow band of dead tissue at the nail base so that it looks less dry, cracked or ragged.\n\nIn grooming routines it is a finishing step after filing, meant to make hands look tended. Clinical research is limited to nail-hydration products generally, so the support is moderate: emollients plainly soften skin, but evidence that oils change nail quality is thin.","mechanism":"Oils act as emollients and occlusives, slowing water loss from the skin of the nail fold and making it more flexible. Some small molecules may penetrate the nail plate slightly, but the plate itself is largely keratin and gains little from surface oil. Regular massage also keeps the true cuticle from lifting or being picked.","risks":"Adverse effects are rare and mostly limited to contact allergy to fragrance or botanical ingredients. The cited study is a manufacturer-funded trial of a different hydrating nail product, and the importance of nail water content in brittleness is itself disputed. Claims that cuticle oil makes nails grow faster or stronger are not backed by controlled evidence; the benefit is cosmetic and requires continued use. Cutting the cuticle is discouraged because it breaks the seal that protects the matrix.","scholarly":[{"title":"Clinical and Instrumental Objective Evidence of the Efficacy of a New Water-Based Nail-Strengthening Solution Containing Pistacia lentiscus and Hyaluronic Acid Applied for Up to 6 Months to Improve the Appearance of Weak, Brittle Nails","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6994637","source":"Dermatology and Therapy","year":2019}],"forums":[],"image":null,"imageCredit":null},"Electric foot file":{"grokipedia":"https://grokipedia.com/page/Pedicure","overview":"An electric foot file is a handheld, battery or mains powered device with a rotating abrasive roller or disc, used to remove calluses and rough skin from the heels and soles. It automates the work of a pumice stone or manual rasp and is typically used on dry skin.\n\nIn appearance care it is used to keep heels smooth and sandal-ready. No clinical trials of these specific devices were found; the rating rests on the long-standing practice of mechanical callus removal, and the visible effect is temporary because the skin thickens again under pressure.","mechanism":"The abrasive head spins and shaves down the thickened stratum corneum of a callus. Because the layer is dead keratin, removal is painless when done lightly, and the skin looks and feels smoother until friction rebuilds it.","risks":"Over-use can remove too much skin, causing soreness, fissures or a rebound thicker callus, and cutting too deep risks infection. People with diabetes, neuropathy or poor circulation are generally advised to avoid home callus removal and use professional care. Podiatry guidance favours combining light filing with moisturisers such as urea creams, and avoiding blades. Heads must be cleaned or replaced between users.","scholarly":[],"forums":[],"image":null,"imageCredit":null},"Korean exfoliating mitt":{"grokipedia":"https://grokipedia.com/page/Exfoliation_(cosmetology)","overview":"The Korean exfoliating mitt (often called an Italy towel or kessa-style mitt) is a rough viscose or nylon glove used on the whole body after soaking in warm water or a bath house. It rubs off visible rolls of loosened dead skin and is a staple of Korean bathing culture.\n\nIn looksmaxxing it is used for smoother, more even body skin and a short-term glow. The dead-skin removal is real and immediately visible, but the effect fades within days as the surface renews.","mechanism":"Warm water softens and swells the stratum corneum, and friction then sheds the outer layers. A small study of this practice found that one scrubbing session temporarily lowered water-holding capacity, which recovered within a few days, while repeated scrubbing shortened stratum corneum turnover time without a measurable change in barrier function.","risks":"The evidence base is small, and mechanical rubbing is known to disturb the barrier. Over-scrubbing can cause redness, micro-tears, irritation and post-inflammatory darkening, particularly on darker skin and on inflamed or acne-prone areas. Sensible use is gentle, infrequent and followed by moisturiser. The satisfying amount of grey rolled skin is mostly dead skin and soap residue, not toxins.","scholarly":[{"title":"Biophysical changes after mechanical injury of the stratum corneum in normal skin","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11918583/","source":"PubMed","year":2002}],"forums":[],"image":null,"imageCredit":null},"Dry brush":{"grokipedia":"https://grokipedia.com/page/Dry_brushing","overview":"Dry brushing is the practice of stroking a stiff natural-bristle brush over dry skin, usually before a shower and moving toward the heart. It draws on Ayurvedic practice and is promoted for smoother skin, better circulation and lymph drainage.\n\nIts looksmaxxing use is as a cheap exfoliant. The entry's rating is limited because physical exfoliation is plausible while the lymphatic, detox and cellulite claims have no good clinical support.","mechanism":"The bristles abrade dead surface cells and briefly increase blood flow to the skin, giving a pink, smoother look. There is no good evidence that it moves lymph in healthy people; clinical manual lymphatic drainage is a separate, trained technique.","risks":"Brushing is generally safe on intact skin if gentle, but should be avoided on rashes, eczema, psoriasis, sunburn, wounds or active acne. Over-brushing damages the barrier. Brushes need regular cleaning. Claims about detoxification and cellulite removal are marketing; the secondary sources reviewed here found no rigorous trials.","scholarly":[],"forums":[],"image":null,"imageCredit":null},"Silicone scar tape":{"grokipedia":"https://grokipedia.com/page/Scar","overview":"Silicone scar tape, or silicone gel sheeting, is a thin adhesive sheet worn over a healing or healed scar. It is used mainly for raised hypertrophic and keloid scars, and is sold over the counter for surgical and acne scars.\n\nThe approach is one of the more widely recommended topical scar treatments and is worn for many hours a day over months. Evidence quality is mixed: Cochrane reviews describe the trials as generally weak, although some data suggest benefit in scar-prone people.","mechanism":"Silicone sheeting is thought to hydrate the stratum corneum and occlude the scar, which reduces fibroblast activity and collagen overproduction, and may also provide light pressure and temperature effects. The exact mechanism is not settled.","risks":"Side effects are mainly rashes, itching or maceration under the sheet, and it should not go over open wounds. The 2013 Cochrane review judged most trials to be of poor quality and could not clearly confirm benefit for prevention or treatment, so improvement may be modest and slow. It will not remove a mature, flat scar.","scholarly":[{"title":"Silicone gel sheeting for preventing and treating hypertrophic and keloid scars","url":"https://cochranelibrary.com/cdsr/doi/10.1002/14651858.CD003826.pub3/information","source":"Cochrane Database of Systematic Reviews","year":2013},{"title":"Silicone gel sheeting for treating hypertrophic scars (protocol)","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6580700","source":"Cochrane Database of Systematic Reviews","year":2019}],"forums":[],"image":null,"imageCredit":null},"Blackout curtains":{"grokipedia":"https://grokipedia.com/page/Curtain","overview":"Blackout curtains are heavy or foam- or film-backed window coverings that block most outside light from entering a bedroom. They are used to darken a room for sleep, particularly for shift workers, in cities with street lighting or in summer.\n\nThe looksmaxxing rationale is that sufficient, regular sleep supports skin, mood and hormone regulation, and darkness aids sleep. Observational and small experimental studies link light during sleep with poorer sleep and metabolic effects, though no data were found for curtains specifically; the benefit is indirect.","mechanism":"Blocking light avoids suppression of melatonin and reduces arousals from light exposure during sleep. A dark room helps maintain the circadian signal that night is occurring.","risks":"Curtains are low-risk. Edge gaps still let light leak, so fit matters. Total darkness can make waking harder in the morning, so some people pair them with a sunrise alarm or open them on waking. Claims that darkness alone repairs skin overstate the evidence; good sleep matters, and the curtain is only one means to that end. Retailer claims about performance should be treated cautiously.","scholarly":[],"forums":[],"image":null,"imageCredit":null},"Sleep mask":{"grokipedia":"https://grokipedia.com/page/Sleep_mask","overview":"A sleep mask is a soft covering worn over the eyes to block light during sleep. Types include flat fabric masks, contoured masks and weighted masks, and it is a cheap and portable alternative to darkening a room.\n\nIt is used for sleeping through light, napping and travel. The best evidence comes from hospital studies, where masks (often with earplugs) improved self-reported sleep, so the rating is strong for light blocking but not specific to appearance.","mechanism":"By blocking light, the mask reduces visual input and light-driven arousals and can help preserve melatonin release. Contoured designs avoid pressure on the eyelids.","risks":"Risks are small: pressure or irritation, skin reactions from materials, and dislike of wearing something on the face. Meta-analyses in intensive care units show benefit mainly in subjective sleep quality, with small trials and variable objective results, so benefits in healthy sleepers at home are inferred. Masks need regular washing.","scholarly":[{"title":"The Efficacy of Eye Masks and Earplugs Interventions for Sleep Promotion in Critically Ill Patients: A Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC8678458/","source":"Frontiers in Psychiatry","year":2021},{"title":"Effect of the use of earplugs and eye mask on the quality of sleep in intensive care patients: a systematic review","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28944590/","source":"Journal of Sleep Research","year":2018}],"forums":[],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6f/Sleep_mask.jpg","imageCredit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sleep_mask.jpg"},"Humidifier":{"grokipedia":"https://grokipedia.com/page/Humidifier","overview":"A humidifier is an appliance that adds water vapour to indoor air. Common types are evaporative, ultrasonic and steam models, and large ones serve whole rooms or houses.\n\nIn skin care it is used in heated winter rooms and bedrooms to reduce the dryness, tightness and flaking that come with low indoor humidity. Field and lab studies link low humidity to lower stratum corneum hydration, but the best target humidity is unsettled.","mechanism":"Raising ambient relative humidity slows evaporation of water from the skin surface, so the outer layer keeps more moisture. Typical indoor recommendations are roughly 30 to 50 percent.","risks":"Poorly maintained units can grow mould and bacteria, and ultrasonic models disperse minerals as white dust unless distilled water is used; they need regular cleaning. Excess humidity encourages mould and dust mites. Effects on visible skin are modest compared with moisturiser, and published thresholds of 30 percent versus 45 percent are not firmly evidenced.","scholarly":[{"title":"Effect of low humidity on the barrier functions of keratinocytes in a reconstructed human epidermal model","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39935117/","source":"PubMed","year":2025}],"forums":[],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e8/Ultrasonic_humidifier.jpg","imageCredit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ultrasonic_humidifier.jpg"},"White noise machine":{"grokipedia":"https://grokipedia.com/page/White_noise_machine","overview":"A white noise machine plays steady broadband sound, such as white, pink or brown noise, fan sounds or nature loops, to mask sudden noises. It is used in noisy bedrooms and for infants.\n\nFor looksmaxxing it serves sleep quality. The evidence is mixed: a 2021 systematic review rated the quality of evidence for continuous noise as very low, with later reviews more favourable in hospital settings.","mechanism":"Continuous sound reduces the contrast between background and sudden noises, so there are fewer arousals triggered by the environment. Pink noise has also been studied for effects on slow-wave sleep.","risks":"Playing noise too loudly may harm hearing, and guidance on infants stresses keeping volume and distance low. Some people find the sound disruptive. Reviews report heterogeneous studies and variable results, so the effect is best seen as helpful for some noisy-environment sleepers rather than universal.","scholarly":[{"title":"Noise as a sleep aid: A systematic review","url":"https://www.em-consulte.com/article/1421191/article/noise-as-a-sleep-aid-a-systematic-review","source":"Sleep Medicine Reviews","year":2021},{"title":"Systematic review: auditory stimulation and sleep","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34964434","source":"Journal of Clinical Sleep Medicine","year":2022}],"forums":[],"image":null,"imageCredit":null},"Cooling eye mask":{"grokipedia":null,"overview":"A cooling eye mask is a gel pad or packet, chilled in a fridge or freezer, that is placed over the eyes. It is used in the morning to reduce temporary puffiness around the eyes.\n\nThe effect is short-lived and cosmetic. No peer-reviewed trial of these masks was found; guidance from eye-care and consumer health sources supports cold compresses for transient swelling.","mechanism":"Cold causes vasoconstriction and slows fluid accumulation, so eyelids look less swollen after several minutes. Gentle pressure from the pad may help too.","risks":"Applying ice directly to eyelids can injure skin, so a barrier and short use are advised. Persistent, one-sided, painful or vision-affecting swelling needs medical review, as it may indicate infection or other disease. It does not treat eye bags that are structural or fat-related.","scholarly":[],"forums":[],"image":null,"imageCredit":null},"Nasal dilator / strips":{"grokipedia":"https://grokipedia.com/page/Nasal_strip","overview":"Nasal strips and internal dilators are adhesive or insert devices that hold the nostrils open. They are used for congestion, sleep and snoring.\n\nIn looksmaxxing circles they are sometimes confused with jaw or face development; they only affect airflow in the moment and do not change facial structure.","mechanism":"A spring-like strip across the nose lifts the nasal side walls and stiffens the nasal valve, lowering airflow resistance while worn.","risks":"Side effects are mostly adhesive skin irritation. A systematic review found no significant change in apnoea-hypopnoea index or snoring index in sleep apnoea, so they are not a treatment for it. Benefits for congestion or snoring in non-apnoea users are mostly subjective.","scholarly":[{"title":"Nasal Dilators (Breathe Right Strips and NoZovent) for Snoring and OSA: A Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5187471","source":"Pulmonary Medicine","year":2016}],"forums":[],"image":null,"imageCredit":null},"Sunrise alarm clock":{"grokipedia":null,"overview":"A sunrise alarm clock (wake-up light) brightens gradually over about 30 minutes before the set wake time, imitating dawn, and may add sound.\n\nIt is used to make waking easier and to support a regular schedule. Trials on dawn simulation show better alertness and mood in some groups, though the evidence is small and mixed.","mechanism":"Gradually rising light through closed eyelids reduces sleep depth and promotes arousal and cortisol rise before waking, easing sleep inertia.","risks":"It is generally safe, though light before waking can disrupt late sleep for some. The only study cited here is an experimental trial in young adults, and an older-adult study found no effect on alertness. A regular wake time itself is a major part of the benefit.","scholarly":[{"title":"Effects of Artificial Dawn and Morning Blue Light on Daytime Cognitive Performance, Well-being, Cortisol and Melatonin Levels","url":"https://orbi.uliege.be/handle/2268/171514","source":"Chronobiology International","year":2013}],"forums":[],"image":null,"imageCredit":null},"Anti-wrinkle pillow":{"grokipedia":"https://grokipedia.com/page/Pillow","overview":"Anti-wrinkle pillows are contoured, cushioned or pillowcase-style products meant to reduce facial compression during sleep, which is thought to contribute to sleep lines on the forehead, cheeks and chest.\n\nThe idea is plausible because sleep wrinkles are linked to repeated pressure, but there are no good trials of these products. Back-sleeping avoids facial contact at no cost.","mechanism":"They try to limit facial compression and shear, for example with cut-outs or pillows that cradle the head, while silk or copper-infused covers aim at friction.","risks":"Risk is low; discomfort and poorer sleep from awkward shape are the main downsides. Product claims are weakly supported and much of the supporting material is marketing. Sun exposure, collagen loss and ageing are larger drivers of wrinkles.","scholarly":[],"forums":[],"image":null,"imageCredit":null},"Tailoring (any clothes)":{"grokipedia":"https://grokipedia.com/page/Tailor","overview":"Tailoring is the alteration of ready-made clothes to the wearer's measurements, such as hemming trousers, taking in shirts or adjusting sleeves. Bespoke or made-to-measure goes further and builds a garment from scratch.\n\nIn style advice, fit is routinely ranked above brand as a factor in appearance. No specific clinical literature was found, and the claim rests on fashion practice and general research on clothing and impression formation.","mechanism":"Alteration removes excess fabric and aligns seams with shoulders, waist and length, so the garment follows body lines instead of hanging or pulling.","risks":"Costs are modest relative to results, but alterations are limited by seam allowance and cannot fix every garment. Poor tailoring can ruin a piece. It is a temporary effect tied to the garments.","scholarly":[],"forums":[],"image":null,"imageCredit":null},"Clothes steamer":{"grokipedia":"https://grokipedia.com/page/Clothes_steamer","overview":"A clothes steamer is a handheld or upright appliance that heats water and sends steam onto hanging garments to relax wrinkles.\n\nIt is used as a faster, gentler alternative to ironing, especially for delicate fabrics. No clinical literature applies; its value is practical grooming.","mechanism":"Hot steam relaxes fibre bonds so the fabric falls smoothly and sets as it dries.","risks":"Steam can burn, and hard water can clog units, so distilled water and descaling help. It is less effective for sharp creases on dense cotton. The benefit is temporary.","scholarly":[],"forums":[],"image":null,"imageCredit":null},"Fabric shaver":{"grokipedia":"https://grokipedia.com/page/Fabric_shaver","overview":"A fabric shaver is a small handheld device with a guarded blade that cuts off pills and loose fuzz from sweaters, knits and upholstery.\n\nIt is used to restore the clean look of worn knitwear. No research literature was found; it is a practical maintenance tool.","mechanism":"Spinning blades under a mesh guard trim fibre balls from the surface while the guard protects the fabric.","risks":"Over-use can thin or hole delicate fabrics, and it should be tested on a hidden area first. It is not for skin or body hair. Pills return as the garment is worn.","scholarly":[],"forums":[],"image":null,"imageCredit":null},"Compression undershirt":{"grokipedia":"https://grokipedia.com/page/Compression_garment","overview":"A compression undershirt is a close-fitting elastic top, often worn under a shirt, that firms the torso outline.\n\nIt is used for a smoother silhouette under fitted clothing. The sports-performance literature on compression garments is separate and shows little performance effect, while the visual smoothing is immediate.","mechanism":"Elastic fabric applies gentle pressure to flatten and redistribute soft tissue, giving a more even surface under clothes.","risks":"Tight garments can be hot and uncomfortable, and overly restrictive ones may limit breathing or movement. The effect is cosmetic and only while worn.","scholarly":[],"forums":[],"image":null,"imageCredit":null},"Collar stays":{"grokipedia":"https://grokipedia.com/page/Collar_stay","overview":"Collar stays are thin rigid strips slipped into pockets under the points of a dress shirt collar to keep them flat and sharp.\n\nThey are used for a crisp neckline. No scholarly evidence exists; it is a tailoring convention.","mechanism":"The stiff strip prevents the collar tip from curling or bending.","risks":"Plastic stays can warp or break and should be removed before laundry or ironing; metal stays last longer. Benefits are small and temporary.","scholarly":[],"forums":[],"image":null,"imageCredit":null},"Shoe trees + polish kit":{"grokipedia":null,"overview":"Shoe trees are inserts, usually of cedar, that hold a shoe's shape when it is off the foot. A polish kit includes brush, cream and wax to clean and shine leather.\n\nThey are used to keep shoes looking neat and extend their life. No specific scholarly sources were found for shoe care, though research on shoes and impression formation exists.","mechanism":"Trees stretch out creases and absorb moisture while the shoe dries, and polish conditions and fills scuffs.","risks":"Poor technique or the wrong polish may stain or dry leather. Some shoes need specific care. The visual effect is temporary.","scholarly":[],"forums":[],"image":null,"imageCredit":null},"Good sunglasses":{"grokipedia":"https://grokipedia.com/page/Sunglasses","overview":"Sunglasses are tinted or polarised eyewear that reduces glare and ultraviolet exposure. Frame shape also changes how the face reads.\n\nIn style advice they are used to balance facial proportions and add polish. The visual effect is instant, though no specific scholarly literature was reviewed here.","mechanism":"Tinted lenses cut visible light and UV, while frame shape, width and bridge alter perceived proportions.","risks":"Poorly rated lenses can let UV in; check UV400 or equivalent labelling. Frames that fit badly are uncomfortable. The effect lasts only while worn.","scholarly":[],"forums":[],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/ad/Black_sun_glasses.jpg","imageCredit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Black_sun_glasses.jpg"}};
